press({ tabId: number, keys: string, repeat?: number })

Press keyboard keys at the current focus — no element target. Use after clicking or typing to commit or navigate: "Enter", "Tab", "Escape", arrow keys, or chords like "cmd+a" / "ctrl+c". Space-separate a sequence (e.g. "Backspace Backspace Delete"). For bulk plain text use keyboard_insert_text; for character typing use keyboard_type.

Native clipboard: use ControlOrMeta+c/x/v to copy/cut the current selection or paste at focus. The extension resolves ControlOrMeta to Cmd on macOS and Ctrl elsewhere; explicit Cmd/Ctrl chords also work. These use the system clipboard and overwrite it when copying/cutting. A completed key dispatch does not verify application content or saving. Let the editor finish processing paste and handle any paste/import dialog before changing selection for read-back; Google Docs processes paste asynchronously. Inspect the destination before retrying a failed paste: it may already have changed.

For prepared rich text, focus the destination editor and select the intended replacement range first. Write text/plain plus text/html through browserjs, then paste. This requires a secure, focused page whose clipboard policy allows writing; propagate a rejected write and use the application's supported import/paste controls if unavailable. For spreadsheet cells, use navigator.clipboard.writeText(tsv) with tabs between columns and newlines between rows, select the destination cells in grid selection mode, then use the same paste shortcut. Verify actual cells, formatting and saved content. Copy between applications with the native copy/paste shortcuts when retaining the source formatting is sufficient; reading the clipboard is unnecessary.

REPL example after preparing destination focus/selection (replaces the system clipboard):
